{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Olivia, the pertinent information is: medical: respiratory and fatigue plan; physical: lifting, positioning, feeding, hygiene; sensory: no primary concern; and behaviour: communicates frustration appropriately. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: fatigue during long blocks; inaccessible tables, sinks, and lab stations; writing volume, not idea generation; and missed instruction during personal-care routines. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Olivia, the most useful EA actions are to follow health and emergency plans exactly; provide dignified personal care and privacy; set up access, then fade unnecessary help; and support peer participation without speaking for Olivia. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-A-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "s